In Git, a branch is like a separate path or notebook where you can work on new ideas or features without disturbing
the main project. Think of the main project as a tidy notebook called "master," where you keep your polished and
finished work. When you want to try something new, you create a new notebook (branch) to experiment freely. If your
new idea works well, you can copy it back into the master notebook. If it doesn’t, you can simply discard that
notebook without messing up your main work.

Imagine you’re writing a story. The master branch is your final draft, neat and ready to share. But you want to
try a different ending, so you create a new branch—a separate draft where you can write and revise without
changing the original story. Once you’re happy with the new ending, you merge it back into the final draft.
This way, branches help you keep your work organized and safe while exploring new possibilities.
